1. Introduction
The increase in the use of fossil fuels has significantly contributed to the growing concerns about anthropogenic climate change. Transportation is a significant cause for this climate change as it accounts for approximately 24 % of the global energy-related CO2 emission [[1], [2], [3], [4]]. Therefore, implementing an active measure for reducing this CO2 emission in the transportation sector is the need of the hour. In response to this challenge, governments and major transportation sectors have turned towards electric vehicles (EVs) as one of the long-term solutions to reduce CO2 emissions [5,6]. Adding value to this point, Fig. 1 shows the year-wise growth of EVs in the global market from 2010 to 2022 and the EVs usage is expected to reach 230 million by 2030 [[7], [8], [9]]. Hence, the production of EVs has been accelerating rapidly over the recent years and has now become a promising step towards achieving a sustainable and eco-friendly environment.
