However, the performance reliability of EVs is also an important factor in evaluating their suitability for widespread adoption [10]. The reliability and lifespan of an EV depend on several critical factors including the motor, battery pack, controllers, and thermal management systems. Fig. 2 schematises the typical sectional view of EV [11]. Due to the technological advancement in EVs, the size of the battery pack and the motor has been reduced, resulting in an increased power density and local hotspot formation. These hotspots can cause heat transportation problems, such as reducing the efficiency of the motor and battery, ultimately leading to vehicle failure. Hence, extensive research addresses the importance of reducing the hotspot formation in EVs, particularly for battery packs [[12], [13], [14], [15], [16], [17], [18]]. However, very little attention has been paid to the issue of hotspot formation and thermal management in EV motors.
