The observations of the EV motor technologies used in commercial EVs through the market and literature survey over the past decade reveal PMSM drives attract manufacturers for their compactness, high power density and high efficiency in regenerative braking. Next to the PMSM motors, IM drives are more significant and combined motor drives and AC SM drives usage are increasing in recent years. Further, the heat-generating losses of the EV drives, being a function of motor temperature and power rating, create heat transport problems and hotspots in EV motors. Thus, the motor's thermal management system is mandated for the reliable performance life of the EV motor.
3. Thermal management techniques in electric vehicle motor
The performance and reliability of the EV motor depend on the cooling system's performance [75]. This section reviews the air and liquid cooling techniques and the influential parameters available in the literature for the thermal management of EV motors.
3.1. Air cooling techniques
Air cooling is the most common method of cooling motors with low power density using natural or forced convection. In an air-cooling system, parameters like airflow path, velocity, fin design and pressure drop across the flow length play a significant role in the efficient cooling of the motor. Hence, this section is devoted to a brief overview of the cooling performance of motors based on the factors mentioned above.
3.1.1. Effect of airflow pattern
Farsane et al. [76] modified the casing design of closed-type induction motors considering the thermal and flow aspects. The modified casing design maintained a uniform airflow with an average temperature drop of 10 °C. Jang et al. [77] observed that introducing a forced air circulation along the internal and external motor surfaces enhances the heat transfer rate of end windings up to 17.5 and 8 %. Li et al. [78] sought to optimise the cooling of an air-cooled 4 kW AC-IM by introducing an axial airflow pattern along the rotor. The optimised airflow pattern reduced the rotor bar temperature by 57 °C, increasing the motor power to 8 kW. Zhu et al. [79] introduced axial-radial cooling ducts along the rotor and stator core of the motor for internal air circulation. The model proposed by the reseachers reduces the PM and stator end winding temperature by 23.7 and 7.4 °C, respectively. Fawzal et al. [80] analysed three different airflow arrangements in an axial flux permanent magnet (AFPM) motor, as shown in Fig. 5, using Computation Fluid Dynamics (CFD). It is observed that design 3 has the minimum pressure drop as the rotor's embedded PM acts like the centrifugal impeller, maintaining a steady state temperature of 86 °C, while designs 1 and 2 maintain 91 and 98 °C, respectively.
