Jaeger et al. [81] studied the thermal management of a motor with an air-cooled rotor along the motor's cooling system and reported a temperature drop of 40 °C and a 50 % enhancement in rated torque. Chen et al. [82] observed a 13.5 °C drop in rotor magnets temperature with dual cycled cooling in a 20 kW fully enclosed PM motor. An internal axial fan was attached to the motor shaft inducing the airflow through the rotor axial vent holes (RAVH) and stator axial vent holes (SAVH), as shown in Fig. 6(a). Asef et al. [83] introduced radial and circumferential airflow duct design along the stator core to enhance the cooling of PMSM, as in Fig. 6(b). The design was analysed with the Lumped circuit and Finite Element Modelling (FEM), and it was reported that the four-duct model has a superior cooling effect than the two-duct model. Kang et al. [84] attached flow deflecting wafters to the rotor, as shown in Fig. 6(c) and improved the design using Taguchi's optimisation method. The experimental and numerical analysis of the wafter design revealed a significant drop in the rotor PM temperature and wind resistance losses of up to 20 and 52 % under various operating conditions. Ai et al. [85] installed and optimised the number and thickness of the flow deflectors to the radial ventilating ducts of the 2500 kW high-voltage motor. The optimised design reduces the maximum temperature of stator windings by 6.3 °C and improves the cooling efficiency to 97.4 % from 97.3 %, saving 5105.7 kW h annually.
