3.1.2. Effect of fin design
Attaching radial fins to the motor surface exists in conventional motor thermal management. Chiu et al. [86] studied the effect of fin number on the cooling performance of a 30 KW SRM motor by attaching radial fins of different numbers (18, 36, 54 and 72) to the motor casing, as shown in Fig. 7(a). The study reported an increase in motor temperature drops with an increase in fin numbers. Ulbrich et al. [87] studied the parameters of the fin design, such as the fin thickness, width, height and number of fins in a segment for the maximum heat transfer, using the 2D Conjugate heat transfer model. The study on fin parameters reveals that fins should be analysed and optimally selected based on the cooling requirement of the motor. Xu et al. [88] designed and optimised a continuous parallel plate fin array for an aircraft taxiing motor. The analyses on fin count, thickness and the back pressure generated in the constrained volume suggested that, reducing the fin thickness to 0.5 mm resulted in a maximum heat dissipation of 2500 W.
