3.1.4. Inferences from air cooling techniques
The research shows that several parameters, such as backpressure, airflow section, air velocity and active heat transfer surface area, influence the effectiveness of the air-cooling technique. Typically, air cooling methods are used in low-power rated motors, say below 10 kW, while they are also used for improving the cooling system efficiency of high-power rated motors [78,79,81,85,94]. Table 2 summarises the results of studies that have investigated the possible methods of improving motor cooling performance using air-cooling techniques. The results highlight that, the arrangements such as SAVH, RAVH, attached rotor impellers, and fins along the motor's surface have also been reported as suitable solutions to enhance the heat transfer rate and offer superior cooling. Studies on volumetric air flow rate and pressure head indicate that airflow is a function of impeller diameter, height, rpm, blade count and design. The results also highlight that the fin count exceeding the optimum value, increases the pressure loss and negatively impacts the cooling performance.

