3.2. Liquid cooling techniques
Liquid cooling is one of the most promising cooling techniques well-suited for motors with high power ratings. It involves liquid coolant circulation through a specifically designed duct or directly within the motor casing. To ensure effective cooling in electric vehicles, it is crucial to utilise coolants with high specific heat capacity and dielectric properties. As a result, water and oil are commonly employed as primary coolants in most EVs. Water exhibits excellent heat absorption and dissipation capabilities, making it an efficient coolant for managing motor temperatures. Additionally, the oil possesses high specific heat capacity and excellent dielectric properties, ensuring both effective cooling and electrical insulation in EV applications. By utilising these coolant options, EV manufacturers can enhance the thermal management of motors, ultimately improving overall performance and reliability. The efficient cooling of the motor through liquid cooling depends on various factors, such as the coolant's quantitative flow rate, pressure, thermal conductivity, viscosity, and dielectric strength. In addition, the design of the cooling system, such as ducts, channels, and heat exchangers, also significantly affect the cooling performance. Therefore, the following section briefly outlines the existing literature on cooling performance of coolant's based on flow rate, temperature and channel design. Optimisation of these factors can enhance the performance, reliability, and longevity of EV motors.
3.2.1. Effect of coolant flow rate and temperature
The amount of heat absorbed by the circulating coolant in a cooling system, depend on its flow rate and the temperature difference between the source and the coolant. Generally, a higher coolant flow rate and lower coolant temperature tend to increase the heat transfer rate and reduce the temperature rise of the motor. Lim and Kim's [97] study on the effect of cooling oil flow rate and temperature highlighted that, the motor winding temperature dropped by 17.17 and 13.5 °C when the flow rate was increased from 1.5 to 2.5LPM and coolant temperature reduced from 65 to 45 °C, respectively. Chiu et al. [86] observed a temperature drop of 30 K with the increase in coolant flow Reynolds number (Re) from 1000 to 4000. Lu et al. [98] observed an exponential drop-in temperature of PM linear motors with water cooling and suggested 2LPM as the optimum flow rate. Davin et al. [99] studied the effect of oil flow rate on different oil spray patterns on the motor windings and observed that the oil flow rate affects the cooling effect depending on the spray pattern used. The results further demonstrated that at higher rpm, the dense oil mist formed with the spray distribution gets distributed along the rotor, increasing the cooling effect. Ye et al. [100] experimentally studied the cooling performance by introducing internal oil flow along the water-cooled motor. A maximum temperature drop of 60 and 68 °C along the end windings were reported for the oil flow rates of 6 and 8LPM, respectively.
Maulidi and Purwadi [101] modelled and analysed the temperature rise of the 30 kW brushless DC motor using the motor losses at different coolant flow rates. The results showed that the motor temperature decreases with an increased cooling water flow rate. Consequently, when the coolant temperature rises from 30 to 40 °C, the coolant requirement quantity increases by five times. Rehman and Seong [102] studied the maximum temperature of the motor at various coolant flow rates in analysing the cooling jacket design of a 90 kW AC-IM. The results showed that the maximum temperature of the motor reduces from 387.2 to 358.5 K, when the coolant flow increases from 5 to 30LPM. Deriszadeh et al. [103,104] analysed various flow rates of the volumetric mixtures of ethylene glycol and aluminium oxide nanofluid with water to improve the cooling performance of motors. The studies on different flow Re showed an increase in heat transfer rate to the cooling system with the rise in flow Re of the coolant. Park and Lee [105] sought to improve motor cooling using modified spiral channel design, observed 29.7 °C drop in motor coil temperature with the increased coolant flow rate from 0.053 to 0.212 kg/s.
Assaad et al. [106] studied the effect of cooling oil temperature in the spray cooling of the motor using the rotor distribution system. The results revealed that the rise in coolant temperature from 60 to 110 °C increases the steady-state temperature of the stator winding from 350 to 361 °C for hollow shaft cooling and 110 to 153 °C for the hollow rotor shaft with an internal projection system. Fujita et al. [107] attempted to cool the electric motor by employing the refrigerant HFC-134a as the working fluid, as shown in Fig. 8. The results revealed that, refrigerant supplied at 0 °C reduced the motor's maximum winding temperature to 53 °C with 60 % higher torque output compared to cooling water at 60 °C.
