Li et al. [114] numerically studied and optimised the cooling channel design parameters, such as the cooling channel's width, height and adjacent width. The study reported an optimum cooling channel width of 10–16 mm and a 5 mm channel height for maximum cooling performance. Rehman and Seong [102] analysed the cooling performance of the radial cooling jacket by varying the number of passes and ports. It was reported that the cooling jacket design with two ports and six passes or above could maintain the motor temperature below the critical temperature limit. Bedkowshi et al. [115] numerically modelled and analysed an in-wheel motor cooling system's stator support structures and coolant flow pattern design to enhance the heat transfer rate. The work involves introducing L-shaped aluminium radiators in the place of thermally conductive resin, observing a maximum temperature drop of 17.2 °C.
Deriszadeh et al. [103,104] analysed the spiral cooling channel design, as shown in Fig. 9(c) with a different number of turns (2 to 8) to remove the heat from the motor. The results reported that the eight-turn spiral cooling jacket has maximum cooling performance regardless of the coolant (aluminium oxide nanofluid and water ethylene glycol mixtures). McCluskey et al. [116] designed a microchannel-based heat exchanger for cooling a 200 kW motor's stator. The results indicate that the microchannel integrated cooling methods can fit aircraft propulsion motors of 1 MW capacity. Tikadar et al. [117] introduced rectangular, circular and direct winding heat exchanger (DWHX) cooling channel designs along the stator core to reduce thermal resistance, as shown in Fig. 10. The studies on the design's cooling effect and magnetic flux interference revealed that the DWHX design has minimum magnetic flux interference and offers superior cooling of 88 to 120 °C compared to 14 to 20 °C and 19 to 29 °C drop on circular and rectangular ducts. Park and Lee [105]. introduced heat sink fins inside the cooling channel and observed enhanced heat transfer rate and reduced average stator coil temperature by 7.2 °C. Wu and EL-Refaie [118] developed an additively manufactured direct drive concentrated winding capable of circulating coolant into the hollow conductor and the manifold. The study revealed that the proposed model maintained the motor temperature below 110 °C, achieving the specific power of 20.1 kW/kg with 95.8 % efficiency. Li [119] introduced spoilers inside the motor cooling water channel, reducing the maximum motor temperature from 325.1 to 320.1 K. Chang and Wang [120] proposed and optimised finned cooling casing with an internal cooling channel design for a 30 kW AFPM motor and observed the reduction in maximum temperature from 111 to 106 °C for a fin height of 33.6 mm. Sixel et al. [121] developed a 3D-printed DWHX-based cooling system for motors and reduced the average motor winding temperature by 90 °C, achieving a motor-specific power of 11.8 kW/kg with 94.7 % efficiency. Fasquelle and Laloy [122] attached water-cooled plates to the magnetic core of the PMSM motor and optimised its design parameters on ambience, coolant flow rate, pressure stack and the distance between the cold plates and attained a temperature drop of 45 °C.
