Xu et al. [124] compared the oil circulation along the stator core windings using the air gap and the different diameter circulating ducts mounted inside the windings of diameter 5, 4 and 3 mm. The flow through the ducts along the windings reduces the maximum temperature by 83.4, 87.4 and 107.6 °C, respectively, for the mentioned circulating ducts compared to oil flow at the intermittent spaces. Park and Kim [125] designed a three-dimensional rotor cooling channel system to distribute oil in an in-wheel motor. The studies on the effectiveness of the proposed distributed system showed 24.9, 11.63 and 15.76 % reduction in the motor temperature relative to the stagnant, circulated and simple channel oil cooling system, respectively. Saleem et al. [126] optimised the oil flow in the rotor duct design of an in-wheel motor using a multi-objective genetic algorithm to reduce the temperature of the direct and indirect spray areas. The optimised design reduced the bearing and resolvers temperature by 8 and 6.4 %, respectively. Marcolini et al. [127] modelled a 25 kW AFPM motor's thermal network and studied the effectiveness of direct oil cooling over the water cooling technique. An improvement of 3.3 and 2 times in heat transfer rate and torque are respectively noted when compared to the water-cooled motor.
Besides the liquid cooling technique using a single coolant, the cooling system using two or more coolants and hybrid cooling techniques require special cooling ducts to facilitate direct and indirect motor cooling. Xueli et al. [128] observed a better cooling effect on a PM motor for the stator cavity filled with an evaporative coolant and the water-cooled casing than the simple water-cooled PM motor. Chuan et al. [129] studied the hybrid cooling of EV motors using water and air to cool the AFPM rotor and stator. The comparative study on temperature rise with the conventional air- and water-cooling technique revealed more uniform torque for the proposed system at all speeds. Silwal et al. [130] studied the temperature distribution in the motor windings for two cooling water jacket designs (WJ1, WJ2), direct coil cooling design (DCC1, DCC2) and two of their combinations (WJ1+DCC1, WJ2+DCC2). The study revealed a maximum motor temperature of 115, 114, 108, 80, 64 and 61 °C for WJ1, WJ2, DCC1, DCC2, WJ1+DCC1 and WJ2+DCC2 configurations. Sugimoto and Kori [131] studied the enhancement in the cooling performance by adding stagnant oil to the PM motor cooling system and observed a 24 % reduction in coil temperature compared to the simple water jacket cooling method. Madonna et al. [132,133] modelled and analysed the enhancement in implementing direct cooling pipes along end windings. The numerical and experimental study of the proposed design showed a 25 % reduction in hotspot formation with a rise in motor torque and current density by 18 and 20 %, respectively. Table 4 summarises the studies on the effect of cooling channel design on electric motor cooling.

