3.2.3. Inferences on liquid cooling techniques
Extensive investigations on liquid cooling thermal management have clarified that liquid cooling techniques offer more reliable temperature control of electric motors than air cooling techniques. It has been observed in the literature that, a decrease in the coolant inlet temperature results in a corresponding drop in the maximum motor temperature. However, it also observed that increasing the coolant flow rate at a constant inlet temperature becomes less significant above the optimum limit. The design of the cooling channel is another critical factor in supporting both direct and indirect cooling of the motor windings. The studies further reveal that the efficiency of the liquid cooling system can be improved by using coolants with higher thermo-physical properties and optimising the cooling channel. Moreover, cooling jackets can also serve as a foundational system that supports the enhancement of auxiliary heat transfer systems. The further review has showed that, by using coolants such as nanofluids and refrigerants, the thermal resistance can be reduced in the cooling channel, leading to enhanced cooling performance for EV motors.
3.3. Passive heat transfer enhancement techniques in EV motors
It is well known that EV motors generate heat during operation, and the excess heat has to be dissipated effectively. However, in some cases, this efficiency gets reduced or limited due to the high thermal resistance in the heat transfer path. Researchers have investigated various passive heat transfer methods to address this limitation, such as implementing phase change materials, potting thermal fillers, embedded conducting rods and HPs to dissipate the excess heat. The following section deals with the existing studies focused on enhancing the heat transfer rate of the cooling system by implementing passive heat transfer devices.
3.3.1. Phase change materials cooling
Phase Change Materials (PCM) thermal management technique in electric motors involves the absorption and dissipation of the heat from the motor parts during motor operation and rest period. Bellettre et al. [134] numerically modelled and analysed the influence of the PCM's filling factor, specific heat capacity and thermal conductivity over the temperature rise of the motor. The studies on using organic P116 and metallic gallium PCM for motor cooling revealed a temperature drop of 14 and 69 °C, respectively, for their difference in thermal conductivity. Wang et al. [135] proposed the PCM-based packing system, as shown in Fig. 12, for a PMSM aircraft actuator with net thermal conductivity of 27.7 W/mK. The study on the proposed design resulted in a 50 % extended operation time with a 12.8 % reduction in peak temperature compared to a conventional fin-based packing system. Wang et al. [136] proposed a paraffin-based PCM-packed casing for the PMSM motor and observed an extended operation time of 212 s in continuous operation using paraffin RT90. Wang et al. [137] modified the PCM cavity design by placing aluminium alloy sheets of different counts (0, 2, 5) and thicknesses (0.5 and 1 mm) inside the PCM blocks. The comparative study of the configurations showed that the PCM package with the five-metal insert of 0.5 mm each is relatively more efficient and increases the operation time by up to 32.7 %. Ayat et al. [138] used RT70HC paraffin PCM-filled hollow conductors for the stator winding and observed an 8 % reduction in the maximum temperature of the winding and an 18 % reduction in winding weight. Liu et al. [139] numerically analysed the temperature reduction in the stator by embedding PCM inside the stator tooth. The test results revealed that under continuous operation, an average temperature drop of 7.5 and 1.2 °C in the stator core and coil, respectively.
