3.3.2. Thermal filler materials
Thermal fillers or potting materials are substances used to fill the air gap between end windings and the casing. These materials facilitate an efficient thermal transfer path, enhancing heat dissipation in the system. Yao et al. [140] analysed the effect of using the Aluminium nitride potting material in 7 kW PMSM and observed a 20.3 °C drop in motor temperature. Polikarpova et al. [141] studied heat transfer enhancement in a 100 kV A AFPM motor using ceramacast 675-N as potting material. The results revealed a 17 °C temperature drop along stator end windings. Polikarpova et al. [142], in a comparative study of ceramacast 675-N and epoxy 2315 as potting material in the stator, reported a maximum temperature deviation of 2 °C due to their difference in thermal conductivity.
Melka et al. [90] covered the stator core with the potting thermal fillers, as shown in Fig. 13(a). The studies on the motor temperature rise with plain and finned casing revealed a temperature drop of 18.5 and 29.8 °C, respectively. Sun et al. [109] experimented with potting silicone gelatine material in a PMSM motor, as shown in Fig. 13(b). The analysis revealed a 27.3 °C temperature drop and doubled peak load operation time. Chai et al. [143] introduced an inner bulge along the stator core of the AFPM motor and impregnated the stator windings with epoxy resins. The results revealed that the motor temperature drop is a linear function of epoxy resin's thermal conductivity and showed a temperature drop of 8.3 °C when using resin with a thermal conductivity of 0.8 W/mK. McCluskey et al. [116] study on the potting material's thermal conductivity revealed that a rise in thermal conductivity considerably reduces the maximum winding temperature. Li et al. [144] analysis on potting the external rotor PM motor end-windings air gap with thermal grease reduced the end-windings temperature by 74 °C and increased the maximum torque from 92 to 115.2Nm. Table 6 summarises the winding temperature data observed by researchers while using the thermal fillers of different thermal conductivity in the motor.
