3.3.3. Embedded conductive materials
The incorporation of conductive material along the motor core and stator teeth serves to reduce heat transfer resistance. This optimization enhances the efficiency of the cooling system by facilitating a more effective heat transfer path. Galea et al. [145] inserted T-shaped copper as a heat path in the stator winding and reduced the maximum temperature from 180 to 112 °C. Polikarpova et al. [146] introduced copper bars along the stator of 75 kW AFPM and achieved a 25 °C temperature drop. Pyrhönen et al. [147] inserted a series of copper rods into the stator core of 75 kW AFPM on both potted and unpotted stators. The studies showed a temperature drop of 16 and 26 °C along the unpotted stator and 14 and 26 °C for the potted stator with single and three copper rods, respectively. Huynh and Hsieh [148] modified the copper windings insulation with materials of higher thermal conductivity and observed that the heat transfer rate increases with the thermal conductance of the insulator. Table 7 summarises the temperature data observed by researchers by adding embedded conductive materials in motors.

