3.3.4. Heat transfer enhancement using heat pipes
Heat pipes (HPs) are passive heat transfer devices exhibiting exceptionally high thermal conductivity. They can be strategically positioned along the motor windings and core to facilitate enhanced cooling. By utilising HPs, heat generated within the motor can be efficiently transferred from the evaporative end to the condensing end. This enables effective heat dissipation and temperature regulation, contributing to improved motor performance and reliability. The high thermal conductivity of HPs allows for efficient heat transfer even over long distances, making them valuable components in motor thermal management strategies. Groll et al. [149] placed HPs along the rotor and stator windings of a 103 kW air-cooled AC-IM and observed a 60 °C temperature drop. Hassett and Hodowanec [150] registered a patent, embedding the straight HP evaporator inside the stator core, winding slots, and external water-cooled condenser, as shown in Fig. 14(a). Owng et al. [151] patented a motor cooling technique with the HP positioned along the RAVH with the condenser attached near the rotor impeller. Ruby et al. [152] patented an integrated single-fluid cooling system EV motor using HP to transfer heat from the motor to the cooling system. Fedoseyev et al. [153] patented a rotor cooling system in a motor with the introduced air-cooled axial rotating HP inside the hollow shaft, as in Fig. 14(b). Putra and Ariantara [154] used an L-shaped flat HP along motor fins, as shown in Fig. 14(c) and achieved a temperature drop of 33.8 °C. Aprianingsih et al. [155] implemented air cooled pulsating HP along the stator core and observed a maximum temperature drop of 84.5 °C along the motor surface. Lian et al. [156] embedded an HP along the electric aircraft's electromechanical actuator dissipating heat into the fuel tank, as in Fig. 14(d), reducing the motor's temperature by 17.8–58.6 % during the flight cycle. Wan et al. [157] introduced the HP along stator end windings dissipating heat to the motor end caps and achieved a 16.4 °C drop in the maximum motor temperature. Wang et al. [158] studied three designs of HPs embedded along the stator end windings and endcaps of PMSM and observed a temperature drop of 9.8, 16.4 and 10.3 °C.
