In monitoring the heat generation and dissipation of the motors, the condition monitoring system collects motor data like temperature, speed, vibration, dielectric spectroscopy, and line leakage current and predicts the failure based on the data [189]. Shu et al. [190] analysed the failure of the motor control components using the fault tree diagnostic techniques to predict the reliability of the EV motor controller. The study reports that surface-mounted components of the controller are prone to failure due to the motor temperature and require periodic replacements for reliable operation. Demetriades et al. [191] developed a real-time thermal model of PM motors and implemented it in a DSP to predict the thermal values of the motor in real-time. The study generates the motor loss values numerically and uses them as model input for the control system. The model-generated thermal values are closer to the experimental result observed. Dong et al. [68,192] further examined the motor's thermal values using the motor loss models and attempted to correct the temperature values based on the real-time thermocouple data input. The real-time data reduced the model's error percentage up to 85 %. Huang et al. [166] used a real-time motor temperature prediction-based cooling system controller for a hybrid HP-enhanced water-cooled PM motor. The proposed model assesses the heat generated and the energy consumption for the motor cooling system, such as pump, fan and operates coolant pump for optimum coolant flow, thus reducing the cooling system energy consumption by 57 %. Table 9 summarises the numerical model and the correlations for the prediction of motor heat loss and heat transfer parameters developed by the researchers.

