The observations from the prior works on the computational modelling of electric motors reveal the potential for reliable prediction of the motor's thermal values. These models can help optimise the design of the motor for better performance and longer lifespan, as well as improve the overall efficiency of the cooling system. Integrating these numerical models with real-time condition monitoring systems makes it possible to accurately predict the motor's temperature and adjust the cooling system's energy consumption. Continued research and development could lead to more accurate models predicting the motor's thermal behaviour with relatively lesser uncertainty under a wider range of operating conditions. In addition, the predictive thermal management techniques integrated with artificial intelligence can be even more efficient and will be worth initiative to provide real-time predictions and recommendations based on the motor's operating conditions.
5. Recommendations for future work
The studies on optimising EV motor technologies and the evolving power rating highlight the need for continuous research and development in motor thermal management systems. The heat transfer and the cooling efficiency of the cooling system depend on the parameters such as coolant flow position, cooling surface design, coolant supply parameters, and thermophysical properties of coolant. Optimisation of these parameters reduces the cooling system energy requirements and increases the heat transfer rate. Studies on advanced cooling techniques, such as phase-change materials, micro-channel cooling, and refrigerant-based cooling, were limited and must be explored well for enhanced cooling and improved heat dissipation and efficiency. Further studies should focus on exploring advanced materials with superior thermal conductivity and heat dissipation properties for enhanced cooling of EV motors.
Computational modelling of the motor helps to predict and optimise the motor's thermal values and performance. Integrating the computational model with the real-time condition monitoring system enables more precise temperature control and enhances motor reliability. Future research should focus on refining and expansion of the computational models to adapt to variable operating and environmental conditions. Further, the expansion of integrated systems towards vehicle systems, such as battery thermal management and cabin cooling, should be explored to develop efficient thermal management solutions for the entire electric vehicle. The study also suggests assessing the environmental impact of the cooling strategy and evaluating the energy efficiency and sustainability of the cooling system and its materials used for motor thermal management to align with the broader goal of sustainable transportation.
The studies also highlight hybridising the motor cooling system with air cooling, HP cooling and potting thermal fillers along the primary cooling system improves the overall heat transfer rate and reliability of the motors used in EVs. Hybridising the motor cooling system with HPs is an increasing area of research interest. However, developing high heat flux dissipating HPs is still underway, as it must fit into the compact space of the tightly packed motor casing. The possible areas of development in the HPs domain can be towards optimising the HP designs to improve the overall thermal efficiency, thereby satisfying the increasing thermal load of EV motors. By pursuing these research areas, it is possible to enhance electric vehicle motors' performance, lifespan, and efficiency and enable more widespread adoption of electric vehicles.
6. Conclusions
The article analyses various motor technologies and strategies for electric vehicles, highlighting the significance of thermal management in ensuring optimal performance and reliability of motors. The effect of various working fluids used for cooling, including air, liquid, refrigerants and passive cooling technologies such as HPs and potting materials, are discussed with their significance on the motor's cooling performance. This review also emphasises the need for ongoing research to develop more effective cooling solutions for the rapidly evolving field of EV technology. The main points that should be considered in the thermal management of EVs motor is summarised as follows.
1.
The thermal management system for an EV motor should be selected considering the motor's power rating and structural constraints. For the motors used for short and intermittent duty cycles, PCM-based thermal management can be a suitable option to maintain stable operating temperatures with sufficient downtime.
2.
In the motor cooling system, the active heat transfer area should be optimised for minimum pressure drop and maximum flow rate within its size constraint. The coolant duct/channel design, flow rate and thermo-physical properties of the coolant play a vital role in determining the cooling system efficiency. Higher pressure drop induced in the coolant flow path by cooling channel design creates uneven coolant distribution, resulting in non-uniform cooling.
3.
Hybridising the cooling system with passive heat transfer enhancers such as potting thermal fillers and conductive rods created a direct conductive heat transfer path to the cooling system, enhancing the heat transfer rate and reducing the motor winding temperature significantly.
4.
Employing HPs in motor cooling systems can dissipate much heat and rapidly enhance the heat transfer rate. HPs used in the EV motor's hybrid cooling system reduce the net energy spent on the cooling system, ultimately enhancing the performance reliability of EV motors.
5.
The thermal modelling of motors assists in predicting the motor's temperature distribution and heat dissipation characteristics. In addition, when integrated with the real-time condition monitoring system, it enhances motor reliability and stabilises motor temperature under the safe operating limit.
